Iraq’s Security Media Cell also denied the reports as “inaccurate”, clarifying that the alert was issued in anticipation of a training routine of Iraqi air forces involving different weapons.
ERBIL, Kurdistan Region of Iraq &ndash; The Iraqi government on Friday denied the closure of the country&rsquo;s airspace after Baghdad&rsquo;s civil aviation authority issued an alert to airmen and provided rerouting instructions while traversing Iraqi skies, citing &ldquo;military activity.&rdquo;
The alert was issued by the Iraqi Civil Aviation Authority (ICAA), according to the US Federal Aviation Administration&rsquo;s official NOTAM (Notice to Air Missions) website, at 09:44 pm Thursday (Baghdad time), informing airmen that standard airways in the country would be restricted daily from 08:00 am (Baghdad time) to 02:00 pm, from November 7 through November 11.

Iraqi Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi sent four military brigades to Basra on Saturday after the government received information that Iran-aligned factions were planning to attack Kuwait from the province, Iraqi security and political sources told MBN on Monday. Baghdad has not announced the reinforcements. But the Iraqi army’s chief of staff, Gen. Abdul Amir Yarallah,&hellip;
The provincial council said the visit focused on securing the border area, preventing potential breaches and strengthening coordination among security and military commanders.
A security source, who spoke on condition of anonymity because he was not authorized to speak to the media, said unidentified factions had planned to launch drones from Basra toward Kuwait and carry out ground attacks along the border if war with Iran broke out anew.
